+<h3 id="wasm">WebAssembly interpreter under Node.js</h3>
+<p>
+The <tt>wasm32</tt> target uses a C bytecode interpreter. JIT compilation,
+FFI and profiling are disabled. It builds a Node.js command
+line executable and a static library with Emscripten. It requires GNU Make,
+a native C compiler, and Emscripten and Node.js with WebAssembly exception
+handling and tail-call support. It has been tested with Emscripten 6.0.3 and
+Node.js 26.5.0.
+</p>
+<p>
+Run these commands from the repository root. Clean the build when switching
+between native and WebAssembly targets or changing compiler flags:
+</p>
+<pre class="code">
+make -C src clean CC=emcc HOST_CC=cc TARGET_SYS=Emscripten
+make wasm
+node src/luajit.js -e 'print(jit.arch, jit.status())'
+</pre>
+<p>
+The build produces <tt>src/luajit.js</tt>, <tt>src/luajit.wasm</tt>, and
+<tt>src/libluajit.a</tt>. Keep the JavaScript and WebAssembly files together.
+The Node.js executable accesses the host filesystem, so scripts and bytecode
+can be loaded by their usual paths. Dynamic C modules are unavailable;
+C API embeddings can link the static library with Emscripten, using
+<tt>-mtail-call -sSUPPORT_LONGJMP=wasm</tt> at compile and link time.
+</p>
+<p>
+Compile and run a bytecode file under Node.js:
+</p>
+<pre class="code">
+LUA_PATH='./src/?.lua;;' node src/luajit.js -b example.lua example.ljbc
+node src/luajit.js example.ljbc
+</pre>
+<p>
+Alternatively, use a native LuaJIT executable built from the same checkout
+with <tt>-bW</tt> to generate 32 bit, non-GC64 bytecode:
+</p>
+<pre class="code">
+LUA_PATH='./src/?.lua;;' /path/to/native/luajit -bW example.lua example.ljbc
+node src/luajit.js example.ljbc
+</pre>
+<p>
+GC64 (<tt>-bX</tt>) bytecode is incompatible with this target. The WebAssembly
+build can use 64 bit host build tools without a multilib compiler.
+Error handling uses
+<a href="https://emscripten.org/docs/porting/setjmp-longjmp.html">Emscripten's
+WebAssembly setjmp/longjmp support</a>. The default allocator and entropy
+source are Emscripten's <tt>realloc</tt> and <tt>getentropy</tt>.
+</p>
+<p>
+Ordinary C functions can run directly from the interpreter's call handlers.
+C API calls to fixed-argument Lua functions can enter the same handlers,
+with direct zero-result and single-result returns to C. These paths retain
+the C frame and error boundary for protected calls and callbacks. Hooks,
+vararg entry, stack growth and other result layouts use the full interpreter.
+</p>
+<p>
+Run the source, stripped and unstripped bytecode, and C API regression tests:
+</p>
+<pre class="code">
+make wasm-test
+HOST_LUAJIT=/path/to/native/luajit make wasm-test
+</pre>
+<p>
+The optional <tt>HOST_LUAJIT</tt> also tests native-generated 32 bit bytecode
+and rejection of GC64 bytecode.
+</p>
+<p>
+To compare interpreter performance against Lua 5.1.1, supply the directory
+containing that release's <tt>lua.h</tt> and C sources:
+</p>
+<pre class="code">
+node tests/wasm/benchmark.mjs /path/to/lua-5.1.1
+</pre>
+<p>
+The baseline builds in <tt>build/wasm-benchmark</tt> without modifying its
+sources. Both interpreters run their own bytecode with <tt>-O2</tt> builds
+under the same Node.js executable. Seven rounds alternate interpreter order;
+each workload gets a fresh process and two warmups. Unrelated workload
+functions are collected before warmup. Checksums must agree, and timing
+excludes startup and bytecode loading with garbage collection enabled.
+The output directory contains <tt>results.txt</tt> with timing and ratio
+tables, and <tt>results.json</tt> with samples, flags and environment details.
+</p>
+<p>
+On macOS, add <tt>--native</tt> to compare these ratios with native Lua
+5.1.1 and LuaJIT's assembly interpreter:
+</p>
+<pre class="code">
+node tests/wasm/benchmark.mjs /path/to/lua-5.1.1 --native
+</pre>
+<p>
+This builds native executables in <tt>build/interpreter-comparison</tt>.
+Native LuaJIT has JIT, FFI and profiling compiled out and uses its default
+allocator and value layout. The report compares both platforms' speedups
+over Lua 5.1.1. Their ratio suggests relative performance headroom;
+allocator, value layout and runtime differences also affect it.
+</p>
 
